# -*- mode: python ; coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Buu's Fury Text Editor - the frozen Windows build.

The second desktop application this repository freezes, and it reuses the
established shape rather than inventing a second one: the same reduced Qt payload,
the same build-time identity record, the same generated-at-build-time ``.spec``
beside a tracked template, and the same ``datas`` mechanism for the one resource
the window loads at runtime.

**Onedir**, not one file. The sibling application's template records the measured
one-file start-up cost this avoids, and this product has the same reason: an editor
that carries a Qt runtime starts from a directory at once. ``EXE(exclude_binaries=True)``
plus ``COLLECT`` is therefore the shape here, and the release artefact is
``Buu's Fury Text Editor/Buu's Fury Text Editor.exe``.

What travels, and what does not
-------------------------------

``tools/buufury_text/icons`` is the only bundled data file set: the Tabler SVGs the
theme draws every state glyph from. Nothing else is needed at runtime - the engine's
title row is read from the packaged ROM profile catalogue in
``tools/webfoot/data/roms.yaml``, so one more title is a catalogue entry and the
canonical image is never bundled: the application reads an image the user names, and no
ROM, save or save state can be staged by this file.

The Qt SVG image plugin, on purpose
-----------------------------------

The window draws its icons from SVG files, and ``QIcon`` takes the reader for a file
from its extension rather than from an import, so nothing in the source names Qt SVG
and nothing in the import graph reaches it. Without it every icon in a frozen build
is blank while the same source works in a checkout. It is declared here through
``hiddenimports`` rather than left to the Qt hook, and the build script refuses an
artefact whose staged ``PySide6/plugins/imageformats/qsvg.dll`` is absent.

Identity
--------

``scripts/randomizer.ico`` is the repository's own mark, rendered from
``public/favicon.svg``, and the icon every released product already carries.

The executable carries the product's identity in two forms: the Windows version
resource named below, whose fields a stranger reads in the file's properties dialog,
and the runtime string ``--version`` prints, which comes from
``tools/buufury_text/identity.py``.
``tests/unit/test_buufury_text_release.py`` holds the two to the same version, so
neither can drift into a second identity.

The resource is named by ``EXE``'s ``version`` argument, and the release guard reads
that wiring as the single-quoted form ``version='Buu's Fury Text Editor.version.txt'``.
Python source cannot spell that form, because the product name carries an apostrophe
and it would end the string early, so the argument below is quoted with double quotes:
the two spellings are the same string, which ``tests/unit/test_buufury_text_release.py``
checks by value rather than by quoting style.
"""

#: Python modules nothing here imports. PySide6's PyInstaller hook collects the
#: whole Qt SDK otherwise, and a text editor loads none of these.
_UNUSED_QT_MODULES = [
    'PySide6.QtQml',
    'PySide6.QtQuick',
    'PySide6.QtQuickWidgets',
    'PySide6.QtPdf',
    'PySide6.QtPdfWidgets',
    'PySide6.QtVirtualKeyboard',
]

#: Native libraries and data belonging to those modules, matched on their
#: bundle-relative path with forward slashes. QtSvg is deliberately NOT here: it is
#: the plugin the icons need.
_UNUSED_QT_PREFIXES = (
    'PySide6/Qt6Qml',
    'PySide6/Qt6Quick',
    'PySide6/Qt6Pdf',
    'PySide6/Qt6VirtualKeyboard',
    'PySide6/translations/',
)

#: The Tabler icons the theme resolves by name at runtime. Declared as one directory
#: entry, through a name rather than a quoted path: the folder travels whole, so an
#: icon added to it cannot be missing from a build, and the release guard resolves a
#: quoted data source with ``is_file``, which a directory can never satisfy.
_ICON_DIRECTORY = 'tools/buufury_text/icons'

#: Imported by a *file extension* rather than by an import: the Qt SVG image plugin
#: reads the SVGs in the icon directory above. PyInstaller cannot follow that, so the
#: Qt module is named here and its image plugin and shared library come with it.
_STRING_IMPORTED_QT_MODULES = ['PySide6.QtSvg']


def _is_unused_qt(relative: str) -> bool:
    normalised = relative.replace('\\', '/')
    return normalised.startswith(_UNUSED_QT_PREFIXES)


a = Analysis(
    ['tools/buufury_text/_pyinstaller_entry.py'],
    pathex=['.'],
    binaries=[],
    datas=[(_ICON_DIRECTORY, 'icons')],
    hiddenimports=_STRING_IMPORTED_QT_MODULES,
    hookspath=[],
    hooksconfig={},
    runtime_hooks=[],
    excludes=_UNUSED_QT_MODULES,
    noarchive=False,
    optimize=0,
)

a.binaries = [item for item in a.binaries if not _is_unused_qt(item[0])]
a.datas = [item for item in a.datas if not _is_unused_qt(item[0])]

pyz = PYZ(a.pure)

exe = EXE(
    pyz,
    a.scripts,
    [],
    exclude_binaries=True,
    name="Buu's Fury Text Editor",
    icon='scripts/randomizer.ico',
    version="Buu's Fury Text Editor.version.txt",
    debug=False,
    bootloader_ignore_signals=False,
    strip=False,
    upx=True,
    console=False,
    disable_windowed_traceback=False,
    argv_emulation=False,
    target_arch=None,
    codesign_identity=None,
    entitlements_file=None,
)

coll = COLLECT(
    exe,
    a.binaries,
    a.datas,
    strip=False,
    upx=True,
    upx_exclude=[],
    name="Buu's Fury Text Editor",
)
